# OBSERVATIONS

1. Node.js and npm installed successfully.
2. Nativefier created a desktop WhatsApp application successfully.
3. WhatsApp Desktop login using QR code worked correctly.
4. Exodus Privacy identified multiple trackers and permissions in WhatsApp.
5. Wireshark captured WhatsApp network traffic successfully.
6. TLS-encrypted traffic protected message contents from direct inspection.
7. DNS requests revealed communication with WhatsApp and Facebook domains.
8. Burp Suite intercepted Facebook web traffic successfully.
9. Facebook used multiple tracking cookies and third-party requests.
10. Browser developer tools revealed analytics and advertising scripts.
11. Facebook breach analysis showed large-scale API-related data exposure.
12. WhatsApp Pegasus case study demonstrated risks of spyware attacks.
13. Email breach analysis demonstrated how compromised accounts can be identified publicly.

---

# RESULT

Privacy audits of Desktop WhatsApp and Facebook were successfully performed using Kali Linux tools such as Wireshark and Burp Suite. WhatsApp communication was found to be encrypted using TLS and End-to-End Encryption, while Facebook demonstrated extensive tracking and cookie usage. Data breach case studies highlighted the risks of insecure APIs, spyware attacks, and weak security practices.
